{
  "version": 1,
  "questionId": "q02",
  "speechText": "For me, I prefer not to rely on only one type of search. In our system, we combine lexical search and semantic search. The main reason is that lexical search is good for exact keywords, while semantic search is better for meaning and similar context. For example, the lexical side uses PostgreSQL full-text search, and the semantic side uses embeddings with pgvector cosine similarity. Because of that, we combine the two rankings with Reciprocal Rank Fusion, or RRF, so a result that performs well across both searches can move higher in the final ranking. At the same time, we also think about failure and security. If the embedding service is unavailable, we can fall back to lexical results, and every search is still limited by the user's access. So overall, the goal is to make retrieval more balanced, reliable, and safe instead of depending on vector search alone.",
